wasmtime 37.0.3: a trap in a program that has loaded libraries no longer aborts the engine.

StoreOpaque::wasm_fault, which the signal handler calls to tell whether a fault is a wasm
out-of-bounds access, asked every instance of the store whether the address is in its memory and
asserted that no two say so. But a shared memory imported into a module is defined by an instance
of its own for each import (SharedMemory::vmimport → generate_memory_export, every time): the
guest program's memory is imported by the program and by each library dylink.rs instantiates, so
with one library loaded two instances claim every address of it. The first wasm trap of such a
program (a write past its memory) panicked in the handler — `assertion failed: fault.is_none()`,
store.rs:2187 — and the engine aborted (panic = "abort"), the whole machine with it, where the
program alone should have got SIGSEGV (addons/mod/0009).

The same change as upstream wasmtime 51.0.0 (51.0.0-rc.1; 50.0.2 still has the assert): the
instances that claim a fault must agree on its wasm address. Checked against the package's
sources of 37, 38, 40, 42, 45, 48, 50.0.2 (old) and 51.0.0-rc.1 (this).

Applied by engine/patch-crates.sh to the published package, in engine/crates (Cargo.toml's
[patch.crates-io]). Drop it with a wasmtime of 51 or later.

--- a/src/runtime/store.rs
+++ b/src/runtime/store.rs
@@ -2181,11 +2181,20 @@
         // If this loop becomes hot in the future, however, it should be
         // possible to precompute maps about linear memories in a store and have
         // a quicker lookup.
-        let mut fault = None;
+        //
+        // A shared memory can be defined by more than one instance in a store,
+        // for example when it's imported through the single-memory instance
+        // that `SharedMemory::vmimport` creates. Each of those instances
+        // reports the same fault, so only assert that the faulting address
+        // doesn't belong to two different memories. The memory size isn't
+        // compared because a shared memory can grow concurrently.
+        let mut fault: Option<vm::WasmFault> = None;
         for (_, instance) in self.instances.iter() {
             if let Some(f) = instance.handle.get().wasm_fault(addr) {
-                assert!(fault.is_none());
-                fault = Some(f);
+                match &fault {
+                    Some(prev) => assert_eq!(prev.wasm_address, f.wasm_address),
+                    None => fault = Some(f),
+                }
             }
         }
         if fault.is_some() {
